%S A400178 360,720,960,1080,1248,1440,1800,1920,2160,2340,2496,2520,2880,3240,
%T A400178 3432,3552,3600,3744,3840,3960,4320,4680,4800,4992,5040,5400,5760,
%U A400178 6120,6240,6480,6720,6840,6864,7020,7104,7200,7488,7560,7680,7920,8280,8640,8736,9000,9360,9600,9720,9984
%N A400178 Integer side lengths of squares formed by constructing three right triangles corresponding to distinct Pythagorean triples on the sides of a triangle.
%C A400178 Possible ordered 6-tuple solution (p,x,y,a,b,c) satisfying the simultaneous equations: p^2 + x^2 = a^2, (p-x)^2 + y^2 = b^2, (p-y)^2 + p^2 = c^2 represent three distinct Pythagorean triples (p,x,a), (p-x,y,b), (p,p-y,c) corresponding to the right-angled triangles formed on the sides of the triangle with integer side lengths a, b and c. The sequence is generated by the integer solutions for p.
%H A400178 Andrew Howroyd, <a href="/A400178/b400178.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400178 Janaka Rodrigo, <a href="/A400178/a400178.txt">Python code for generating 6-tuple of solutions of A400178</a>.
%e A400178 First valid ordered 6-tuple solution (360,105,136,375,289,424) represents the three distinct Pythagorean triples (360,105,375), (255,136,289), (360,224,424) corresponding to the right-angled triangles constructed on the sides of the triangle of side lengths 375, 289 and 424. Therefore, the first term of the sequence is 360.
%e A400178 The first example of a p corresponding to more than one solution is 2880 with Pythagorean triples (2880,840,3000), (2040,1088,2312), (1792,2880,3392) and also (2880,2160,3600), (720,483,867), (2397,2880,3747).
%o A400178 (PARI) isok(p)={my(c=0); fordiv(p^2, d, my(x=(d-p^2/d)/2); if(x>0 && !frac(x) && x<p, my(q=p-x); fordiv(q^2, e, my(y=(e-q^2/e)/2); if(y>0 && !frac(y) && y<p && x<=p-y && issquare((p-y)^2+p^2), c++)))); c} \\ _Andrew Howroyd_, Oct 22 2026
%Y A400178 Cf. A046083, A046084, A009000.
%K A400178 nonn,new
